 $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ the magnitude of its acceleration is ${2}^{2-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and the acceleration is directed toward $O.$ If $P$ has a displacement of $1\\,m$ when its velocity is $4\\,m/s,$ find $v$ in terms of $x.$" 13194139535025 true] [17592186327517 1113 #uuid "a6d10a0c-9eaf-5b8d-b65b-c7a48f4d43e0" 13194139535025 true] [17592186327517 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049128/assets/6ddbfef68a0b41a6ee3f11c46ff7821f7ff0dc23a16b4244b5e7a1f6579d7dd2.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude ${2}^{2-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-{2}^{2-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-{2}^{2-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (-{2}^{2-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={2}^{2-x}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=1\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=4\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={2}^{2-x}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & ={2}^{2-1}+C \\\\ 8 & =2+C \\\\ C & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={2}^{2-x}+6 \\\\ {v}^{2} & ={2}^{3-x}+12 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =4({2}^{1-x}+3) \\\\ v & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{{2}^{1-x}+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=2\\sqrt{{2}^{1-x}+3}.\n$$" 13194139535025 true] [17592186327518 1